# E-commerce Backend – Project Context

## Stack
- **Runtime**: Node.js
- **Framework**: Express 5
- **DB**: MongoDB with Mongoose
- **Auth**: API key (x-api-key) + permission middleware, JWT (access + refresh), bcrypt

## Layout
- `server.js` – entry; starts app, graceful shutdown (SIGINT/SIGTERM)
- `src/app.js` – Express app, middleware, routes, global error handler
- `src/routes/index.js` – API key + permission('0000'), mounts `/v1/api` (e.g. access)
- `src/routes/access/` – auth routes (signup, login)
- `src/controllers/` – thin; call services, send SuccessResponse/CREATED
- `src/services/` – business logic (access, shop, keyToken, apikey)
- `src/models/` – Mongoose schemas (Shop, KeyToken, Apikey)
- `src/auth/` – checkAuth (apiKey, permission, asyncHandler), authUtils (createTokenPair)
- `src/core/` – success.response (SuccessResponse, CREATED), error.response (BadRequestError, etc.)
- `src/configs/config.mongodb.js` – dev/pro env; exports selected config (app.port, db.host/port/name)
- `src/dbs/init.mongodb.js` – singleton MongoDB connection
- `src/helper/check.connect.js` – checkConnect, checkOverload, stopCheckOverload
- `src/utils/` – getInfoData (lodash pick), httpStatusCode (StatusCodes, ReasonPhrases)

## Conventions
- Use existing error classes from `src/core/error.response` and pass errors to `next()` so the global handler can respond.
- Wrap async route handlers with `asyncHandler` from `src/auth/checkAuth`.
- Use `SuccessResponse` / `CREATED` in controllers; avoid raw `res.status().json()` for success.
- New API routes: add under `src/routes/` and mount in `src/routes/index.js` (already behind apiKey + permission).
